## What SOLID Stands for in Software Design

**SOLID** is a mnemonic acronym for five foundational design principles in object-oriented software engineering. These principles were originally introduced by Robert C. Martin (Uncle Bob) and later formalized as the **SOLID** acronym by Michael Feathers around 2004 to help developers write code that is easier to maintain, scale, and reason about. [1](#2105578a-dfa7-40ef-b8aa-abaf1a34143a) [2](#2105578a-dfa7-40ef-b8aa-abaf1a34143a)

The five letters stand for:

- **S — Single Responsibility Principle (SRP):** A class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should take on just one responsibility and handle it well. [1](#2105578a-dfa7-40ef-b8aa-abaf1a34143a) [3](#2105578a-dfa7-40ef-b8aa-abaf1a34143a)

- **O — Open/Closed Principle (OCP):** Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification, allowing new behavior to be added without altering existing code. [2](#2105578a-dfa7-40ef-b8aa-abaf1a34143a) [4](#2105578a-dfa7-40ef-b8aa-abaf1a34143a)

- **L — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP):** Objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of a subclass without breaking the correctness of the program. [2](#2105578a-dfa7-40ef-b8aa-abaf1a34143a) [3](#2105578a-dfa7-40ef-b8aa-abaf1a34143a)

- **I — Interface Segregation Principle (ISP):** Clients should not be forced to depend on interfaces they do not use; prefer smaller, specific interfaces over large, general-purpose ones. [2](#2105578a-dfa7-40ef-b8aa-abaf1a34143a) [4](#2105578a-dfa7-40ef-b8aa-abaf1a34143a)

- **D — Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP):** High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; instead, both should depend on abstractions rather than concrete implementations. [2](#2105578a-dfa7-40ef-b8aa-abaf1a34143a) [3](#2105578a-dfa7-40ef-b8aa-abaf1a34143a)

